Optimise for straightforward, maintainable code. Implement the smallest complete solution that meets the stated requirements.

1. Understand before changing.
    Read the relevant code and trace the existing behaviour. Reuse established patterns. Fix problems at their source.
2. Build only what is required now.
    Apply YAGNI: “You Aren’t Gonna Need It.” Do not add speculative features, configuration options, extension points or infrastructure for hypothetical future needs.
3. Reuse before inventing.
    Prefer suitable existing code, standard libraries, native platform features and installed dependencies. Add a dependency only when it clearly reduces the total maintenance burden.
4. Make abstractions earn their place.
    Every new layer, wrapper, service or generic helper must solve a concrete problem. A little duplication is acceptable when the alternative couples unrelated behaviour or introduces premature generalisation.
5. Keep logic easy to follow.
    Use descriptive names, cohesive functions, early returns and shallow nesting. Avoid clever one-liners, tangled conditions and hidden side effects. Extract helpers when they clarify a meaningful operation.
6. Keep state and data flow simple.
    Give each piece of information one authoritative source. Derive values where practical instead of storing and synchronising duplicate state.
7. Keep changes focused.
    Avoid unrelated refactors and rewrites. Remove code made obsolete by the change. Preserve existing behaviour outside the requested scope.
8. Preserve correctness.
    Keep necessary validation, permissions and error handling. Never silently swallow failures or use fake success fallbacks. Test important behaviour and realistic failure cases; add regression coverage for bugs.
9. Review for unnecessary complexity.
    Before finishing, inspect the diff for avoidable branches, duplicated state, redundant wrappers, unused code and unjustified dependencies. Simplify where behaviour remains correct, then run the relevant checks.

Briefly explain what changed, why any added complexity is necessary, and what was verified. Stop when the requirements are satisfied.